"Combines residual size and predictor leverage to identify observations that may strongly affect a fitted regression.\n\nUse when: A regression needs investigation of influential observations. Extreme predictor combinations and unusual responses should be distinguished.\n\nAvoid when: The plotted leverage or residual definition is unsuitable for the fitted model.\n\nVisual grammar: Horizontal position encodes leverage. Vertical position encodes standardized or studentized residual; contours or point area may encode Cook’s distance.\n\nBuild: Calculate leverage, the named residual statistic, and influence measures from the fitted model. Draw residuals against leverage with zero and contextual reference guides. Label only candidate observations and keep labels outside points and contours.\n\nCheck: Check leverage and influence are calculated from the same fitted sample. Verify the model’s intercept and parameter count are handled correctly. Name any Cook’s-distance guide values as diagnostic references.\n\nPitfalls: High leverage alone does not mean an observation is influential. Contour crossings and point overlap encode diagnostic quantities; use an influence table when reading labels becomes unreliable.\n\nExample: Inspect a synthetic cost regression to distinguish an unusual predictor record with small residual from one that substantially changes the fit.\n\nAlternatives: Cook’s distance plot, Residual plot, Scatterplot."
